    <packetoffset>0x06</packetoffset>
    <range low="-48.000000" high="143.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>19</datatype>
    <unittype>15</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X*.75-48">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="TPSVOLTAGE" idhash="0xB0752AF0" title="TPS Voltage">
    <desc>followed behaviour I expected for tps voltage from a preveous project that read tps voltag.&#013;&#010;goes high when tps sensor disconnected.&#013;&#010;Same byte as the KSW_dmg210 adx.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to Claude the ram address is &#009;&#013;&#010;0xF8AB</desc>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>TPS Voltage (V)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x07</packetoffset>
    <range low="0.000000" high="5.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>1</datatype>
    <unittype>32</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X * 5 /255">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="THROTTLEPOSITI" idhash="0x33450280" title="Throttle Body Position">
    <desc>Responed to applyting throttle, typically max raw value in logs is 200, so assumed equation was X/2, but some logs were higher for some reason. Same byte as KSW_dmg210 adx but diffrent equation.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to Claude the ram address is 0xF8A9&#013;&#010;</desc>
    <flags>0x00000004</flags>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>Throttle Body Position (%)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x08</packetoffset>
    <range low="0.000000" high="100.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="100.099998" />
    <digcount>1</digcount>
    <outputtype>1</outputtype>
    <datatype>57</datatype>
    <unittype>67</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X*.5">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="RAW_09" idhash="0xEF441CC6" title="0x09 Closed Throttle Offset ?">
    <desc>From logs seemed to have some relation to tps, but I couldnt figure out exact meaning.&#013;&#010;Updated about every 5 frames. when tps was unplugged it jumps to 16384, then decayed toward raw value of tps voltage*~256&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude it is &quot;TPS closed-throttle offset&quot;, ram address&#009;&#013;&#010;0xF960-0xF961&#013;&#010;it said it had high confidence for what this value was. but i havent been able to confirm</desc>
    <flags>0x00000002</flags>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>0x09 Closed Throttle Offset ? (TPS A/D counts x256)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x09</packetoffset>
    <sizeinbits>16</sizeinbits>
    <range low="0.000000" high="16384.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>3</datatype>
    <unittype>3</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="MAFVOLTAGE" idhash="0xB18DC2F0" title="Mass Air Flow Voltage">
    <desc>Moved with rpm and throttle, disconnecting maf made it go to 255, maf voltage seemed like best bet. Same as KSW_dmg210 adx.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude the ram address is 0xC55D</desc>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>Mass Air Flow Voltage (V)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x0B</packetoffset>
    <range low="0.000000" high="5.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>1</datatype>
    <unittype>32</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X*5/255">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="MAFREADING" idhash="0x3C1AED99" title="MAF Air Mass flow rate">
    <desc>Moved with maf voltage, guessed it was measured flow rate so set equation so output would match ID_MAF_TAB, followed closely enough that Im confident it is maf.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude the ram address is 0xF8EC-0xF8ED</desc>
    <flags>0x00000002</flags>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>MAF Air Mass flow rate (kg/h)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x0C</packetoffset>
    <sizeinbits>16</sizeinbits>
    <range low="0.000000" high="600.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>26</datatype>
    <unittype>13</unittype>
    <MATH equation="0.25 * X">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="ENGINELOAD" idhash="0x5A489056" title="Engine load, MAF scale*">
    <desc>Seemed to closely follow maf flow rate (0X0C-0X0D) but also varied with rpm, figured out it was maf/rpm, not entireley sure about equation its mostly a guess.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude &quot;Verified in the firmware: written at 0x79072 from the air-mass value F8EE divided by rpm (F968). It is the load value on the ECU&apos;s &apos;MAF&apos; scale: the OBD calculated-load routine (0x685E2) normalises it by C_MAF_MAX (raw 101 = 550 kg/h, i.e. 5.447 kg/h per high-byte count, or about 0.0212 per LSB of this 16-bit word, the same factor the XDF uses for 16-bit MAF axes). So full load is about 25856 (101*256).&#013;&#010;Correction: the unit is the XDF &apos;MAF&apos; scale (kg/h equivalent), not mg/stroke. The equation is a guess because the exact physical scaling of this word is not proven&quot;&#013;&#010; ram address 0xF8F4-0xF8F5&#013;&#010;Confidence: medium-high for what it is, medium for the scale.</desc>
    <flags>0x00000002</flags>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>Engine load, MAF scale? (0 if MAF unplugged) (raw)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x0E</packetoffset>
    <sizeinbits>16</sizeinbits>
    <range low="0.000000" high="32768.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>1</outputtype>
    <datatype>2</datatype>
    <unittype>90</unittype>
    <MATH equation="0.021194781*X">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="RAW_10" idhash="0xEF45C9A6" title="Filtered engine load*">
    <desc>closely matched Engine load, but valuies were slightly different, seemed to be a smoothed value but kept working when maf was unplugged.&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude &quot;Verified in the firmware: written at 0x6458E. It is a first-order low-pass (routine A5AC) of 0x0E-0x0F (F8F4) with a time constant taken from IP_MAF_CRLC_PU__N / IP_MAF_CRLC_PL__N (rpm dependent). When the MAF diagnosis fails it is replaced by the substitute byte C55A shifted left 8 bits, which is why it keeps working with the MAF unplugged and then only takes multiples of 256.&#013;&#010;Same scale as 0x0E-0x0F&quot;.&#013;&#010;ram address 0xF8EA-0xF8EB&#013;&#010;Confidence: high.</desc>
    <flags>0x00000002</flags>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>Filtered engine load (smoothed 0x0E-0x0F) (raw)</units>
    <packetoffset>0x10</packetoffset>
    <sizeinbits>16</sizeinbits>
    <range low="0.000000" high="32768.000000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>1</digcount>
    <outputtype>1</outputtype>
    <datatype>3</datatype>
    <unittype>3</unittype>
    <MATH equation="0.021194781*X">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

  <ADXVALUE id="RAW_12" idhash="0xEF45DD46" title="maf altitude correction *">
    <desc>Seemed consistent from the end of one log to the start of the next, resetting adaptive values caused it to start at 128 for the next log, settled to around 110-112, only moved in steps of 1, couldnt figure out exact meaning&#013;&#010;&#013;&#010;according to claude &quot;Verified in the firmware: high byte of the filtered 8.8 value F8F8 (low-pass with C_MAF_FAC_ALTI_CRLC, limited by C_MAF_FAC_ALTI_MMV_MIN/MAX, routine at 0x64768). It is the MAF altitude (air-density) correction factor MAF_FAC_ALTI_MMV, used as the axis of ldp_maf_fac_alti_mmv_ip_isapwm (axis 90..128) and the IP_*_ALTI_* tables.&#013;&#010;Equation: 128 = 1.00 (the XDF uses 0.0078125*X for every 128 = 1.0 factor), so X/128. Logs show 104..128 = 0.81..1.00.&quot;&#013;&#010;ram address 0xC558&#013;&#010;Confidence: high for the identity, medium for the scaling.</desc>
    <parentcmdidhash>0xDAA83ACD</parentcmdidhash>
    <units>maf altitude correction *</units>
    <packetoffset>0x12</packetoffset>
    <range low="0.500000" high="1.500000" />
    <alarms low="0.000000" high="255.000000" />
    <digcount>2</digcount>
    <outputtype>3</outputtype>
    <datatype>3</datatype>
    <unittype>3</unittype>
    <MATH equation="X / 128">
      <VAR varID="X" type="native" />
    </MATH>
  </ADXVALUE>

